Q: Can Shadcn ui be installed for Vite + React with javascript and not typescript? A: <p>TypeScript is the recommended way but they provide components in JavaScript version too which you can use by:</p>
<ol>
<li>create <code>jsconfig.json</code> in root folder instead of <code>tsconfig.json</code> and paste the below content:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code> {
  &quot;compilerOptions&quot;: {
    &quot;baseUrl&quot;: &quot;.&quot;,
    &quot;paths&quot;: {
      &quot;@/*&quot;: [&quot;./src/*&quot;]
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>Update your <code>vite.config.js</code> code with below content:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { defineConfig } from &quot;vite&quot;;
import react from &quot;@vitejs/plugin-react&quot;;
import tailwindcss from &quot;@tailwindcss/vite&quot;;
import path from &quot;path&quot;;
import { fileURLToPath } from &quot;url&quot;;

const __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
const __dirname = path.dirname(__filename);

// https://vite.dev/config/
export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],
  resolve: {
    alias: {
      &quot;@&quot;: path.resolve(__dirname, &quot;./src&quot;),
    },
  },
});
</code></pre>
<ol start="3">
<li>Run on terminal (Verifies everything and initialize shadcn)</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>npx shadcn-ui@latest init
</code></pre>

Q: Can&#39;t connect to Neon database using Sequelize A: <p>I found the solution. I had to add this in my file:</p>
<pre><code>import net from 'net';

net.setDefaultAutoSelectFamily(false);
</code></pre>

Q: Does @Order on a @Configuration class determine SecurityFilterChain precedence in Spring Security? A: <p>It's important to understand that the <code>@Order</code> annotation affects <strong>only</strong> the order in which beans of the same type are <strong>collected into a collection</strong>.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Configuration
public class Config {

    @Bean
    @Order(2)
    public A aImplFirst() {
        return new AImplFirst();
    }

    @Bean
    @Order(1)
    public A aImplSecond() {
        return new AImplSecond();
    }
}

public interface A {}
public class AImplFirst implements A {}
public class AImplSecond implements A {}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Autowired
    private List&lt;A&gt; list;

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) throws Exception {
        list.forEach(System.out::println); 
        // Output: 
        // AImplSecond
        // AImplFirst
    }

    static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>However, if <code>@Order</code> is placed on the configuration class itself, it affects only the configuration bean, but not the beans declared inside it. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Configuration
@Order(2)
public class ConfigOne implements ConfigI {
    @Bean
    public A aImplFirst() {
        return new AImplFirst();
    }
}

@Configuration
@Order(1)
public class ConfigTwo implements ConfigI {
    @Bean
    public A aImplSecond() {
        return new AImplSecond();
    }
}
@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Autowired
    private List&lt;A&gt; listA; // collect all 'A' beans

    @Autowired
    private List&lt;ConfigI&gt; listConfigs; // collect all 'ConfigI' beans

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) throws Exception {
        listA.forEach(System.out::println);
        // Output (no impact on the order of beans of type A):
        // AImplFirst
        // AImplSecond

        listConfigs.forEach(System.out::println);
        // Output (Order has impact on the order of beans of type ConfigI):
        // ConfigTwo
        // ConfigOne
    }

    static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>So, answering your questions, the <code>@Order</code> annotation on the <code>@Configuration</code> class does <strong>not</strong> affect the precedence of the <code>SecurityFilterChain</code> beans produced by that configuration. In your case, the correct approach is to put <code>@Order</code> on the <code>SecurityFilterChain</code> beans themselves.
Under the hood, these <code>SecurityFilterChain</code> beans are collected and autowired as a list (in my 4.1.0 version of spring boot, the List is autowired into <code>WebSecurityConfiguration</code> class) and then applied in that order.</p>
<p>You can find more info in this <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/core/annotation/Order.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">doc</a>.</p>

Q: How to do a tls handshake with &quot;api.steampowered.com&quot; using boost asio? A: <p>The server requires Server Name Indication (SNI) and certificate verification, which your current code is missing. That <code>tlsv1 alert internal error</code> usually means the server dropped the connection because it doesn't know which certificate to show you.</p>
<p>You'll need to set up the context and the native handle before calling <code>async_handshake</code>. I'd do it like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>boost::asio::ssl::context ctx(boost::asio::ssl::context::tls);

ctx.set_default_verify_paths();
ctx.set_verify_mode(boost::asio::ssl::verify_peer);

auto connection = boost::beast::ssl_stream&lt;boost::beast::tcp_stream&gt;{ executor, ctx };
get_lowest_layer(connection).expires_never();

std::string const host = &quot;api.steampowered.com&quot;;
std::string const port = &quot;443&quot;;

auto results = co_await resolver.async_resolve(host, port, boost::asio::use_awaitable);
co_await get_lowest_layer(connection).async_connect(results, boost::asio::use_awaitable);

if(!SSL_set_tlsext_host_name(connection.native_handle(), host.c_str()))
    throw boost::system::system_error(static_cast&lt;int&gt;(::ERR_get_error()), boost::asio::error::get_ssl_category());

connection.set_verify_callback(boost::asio::ssl::host_name_verification(host));

co_await connection.async_handshake(boost::asio::ssl::stream_base::client, boost::asio::use_awaitable);
</code></pre>
<p>So, the main thing is calling <code>SSL_set_tlsext_host_name</code> on the native handle. Without that, the Steam API won't know how to handle the TLS handshake. Also, <code>boost::beast::ssl_stream</code> is actually deprecated now, so I'd move to <code>boost::asio::ssl::stream</code> when you get the chance.</p>
